      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for your review, I checked into both:\n\n1. Force-reboot vs. reboot-guard: You\u0027re right. force-reboot.service has Requires\u003dreboot-guard-disable.service and After\u003dreboot-guard-disable.service before running ExecStart\u003d/sbin/reboot, so it runs reboot-guard-disable first and disarms the guard prior to rebooting. The guard can\u0027t stop this reboot path.\n\n2. Do other update services arm the guard? No. The reboot-guard is only armed by the BMC image updater (bmc/ActivationBlocksTransition::enableRebootGuard()). The common/ device-update framework (VR, CPLD, etc.) never touches it — it only exposes the xyz.openbmc_project.Software.ActivationBlocksTransition D-Bus interface (set around the write in Device::startUpdateAsync()).\n\nBecause device updates don\u0027t arm the guard and force-reboot disarms it anyway, the systemd guard doesn\u0027t cover concurrent updates today.\n\nA guard-based fix would require reworking common updaters to arm it and altering rebootBmc(). But because reboot-guard is a single non-ref-counted toggle, one update finishing would disarm it for all others, creating race conditions.\n\nKeying off the ActivationBlocksTransition D-Bus marker avoids these limitations entirely and gives us clean \"defer until peers finish\" semantics. Given these constraints, the D-Bus marker is the robust path here.",
